.TH "SKARN-VET" "1" "2026-06-23" "skarn 0.35.0" "Skarn Manual"
.SH NAME
skarn\-vet \- Vet the local AI config, read\-only and offline. Free, no
license
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBskarn\fR \fBvet\fR [options]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Statically vet the local AI assistant configuration (hooks, MCP servers,
permission grants, plugins and skills) for risky patterns across Claude Code,
Codex CLI, Cursor, Copilot, Copilot in Code, Gemini CLI, Grok Build, Grok Bot,
and Microsoft Scout. Read\-only and offline; free, no license.
.PP
Statically vet this machine's AI assistant configuration \- hook commands, MCP
server definitions, permission grants, and installed plugins and skills across
Claude Code, Codex CLI, Cursor, Copilot, Copilot in Code, Gemini CLI, Grok
Build, Grok Bot, and Microsoft Scout \- and report the risky patterns it
finds: hooks that exfiltrate local content or fetch\-and\-execute remote code,
hooks that post the session event envelope to a remote http endpoint, hooks
that rewrite an assistant's own config, MCP endpoints on remote hosts, MCP
launchers with no pinned version or digest, permission grants that approve a
whole class of actions, grants that let the assistant ask to widen its own
authority, and extensions installed from a remote source or carrying no
integrity marker. Two more patterns come from permission metadata and declared
posture flags alone: a plaintext credential store whose file mode grants group
or other read, and an assistant configured with an always\-on local\-exec and
egress channel. Vet reports declared configuration, not the effective merge
across layers, so a grant a higher layer denies, or a permission mode an
administrator lock disables, is still surfaced: the declaration exists and is
one edit away from active. Project configuration is read at the cwd\-level
file only, so a host that also loads configuration from parent directories is
covered there and no further. A hook entry's command, bash and powershell
fields are each vetted, and the hook rules also match the PowerShell forms of
a download, an upload, an execution and a write, in any letter case; a command
passed through \-EncodedCommand or an abbreviation of it is flagged as
dangerous and is not decoded. Vet is read\-only and offline: it opens each
config file it reads for reading, never writes one, and makes no network call.
A config file that does not exist is skipped; a config file that exists but
cannot be read is a coverage gap that fails closed under a \-\-fail\-on flag.
Each per\-assistant configuration file listed below whose contents vet reads
is opened once, and its permission metadata is read off that same handle. The
stat\-only stores are the exception, because their contents are never wanted:
Grok Bot's sand\-secrets.json, the Copilot CLI's data.db and each Copilot in
Code profile's data.db, whose file mode alone decides a finding, and Grok
Bot's local\-exec\-daemon\-connection.json, whose presence alone does, are
measured by a stat that does not follow a symlink and are never opened, so a
symlinked store is reported as a coverage gap named SymlinkNotFollowed rather
than measured through its target. Every other config file is still read
through a symlink, so a dotfiles\-managed configuration keeps its coverage. A
path that is not a regular file is named as a coverage gap rather than read.
The config\-file count the report prints, and the configFilesRead field in the
json and sarif output, count every such file vet examined, including those
stores. The hook directories and extension manifests vet also walks keep their
own older read path. A JSON config that repeats a key is read both ways, as
the reader that keeps the first value sees it and as the one that keeps the
last does, every finding either reading carries is reported, and the
repetition itself is reported as vet\-config\-duplicate\-key at medium, never
as a coverage gap, so \-\-fail\-on\-severity medium now exits 1 on such a file
where it used to exit 6. Vet also reads the env block and the headers block
(Codex's http_headers table) of every MCP server definition in the files
above, whatever the server's transport, and reports
vet\-mcp\-plaintext\-credential when a value carries a credential as a
literal: high when a detection rule from the embedded pack matches inside the
value, naming that rule, and medium when no rule matches but the key is
secret\-named and the whole value is a secret\-shaped literal. A value that is
entirely a reference to an environment variable, a secret manager or a file
path, spaces included, is not reported unless it embeds a credential the
detection rules recognize, a dotted TOML env key, a TOML value that continues
on a later line, any other mcp_servers line the TOML reader cannot read whole
(an unbalanced bracket, a root\-level or bare\-section mcp_servers assignment,
a table nested under env) and a Grok Build env sub\-table stay coverage gaps,
and the stat\-only stores are still never opened. Only a redacted preview of
the matched value is printed, and every matched value is scrubbed from every
field of every finding in the report and from every coverage\-gap path and
reason, in every spelling it can take there (case, percent\-encoding, control
masking), overlapping matches masked as one span, and every path, a finding's
config path and a coverage path alike, is scrubbed before it is masked or
shortened for display. If the embedded rule pack cannot be loaded, that is a
coverage gap that the stderr notice names and the other rules still run; if
the gap itself cannot be recorded, vet exits 6. Vet surfaces risky
configuration and does not change it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fIfmt\fR
Output format. json and sarif carry the same rule ids, severities, config
paths, and locators as the text list. One of: text, json, sarif. The default
is text.
.TP
\fB\-\-fail\-on\-severity\fR \fIlevel\fR
Exit 1 if any finding is at or above this severity. Without it, vet always
exits 0: it surfaces, it does not gate. One of: low, medium, high, critical.
.TP
\fB\-\-fail\-on\-scan\-error\fR
Fail closed with exit 6 when a config file that exists was not read: it could
not be read, or it was skipped because it is not that assistant's live
configuration. A partial view of the configuration is never reported as clean.
Implied by \-\-fail\-on\-severity. A home directory that cannot be resolved at
all exits 6 regardless of this flag, since nothing was examined.
.TP
\fB\-\-offline\fR
Disable all network access. Vet never makes a network call; this is the
belt\-and\-suspenders guarantee.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR
Disable colored output.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fB~/.claude/settings.json, ~/.claude/settings.local.json, ./.claude/settings.json, ./.claude/settings.local.json\fR
Claude Code hooks and permission grants. Read, never written. The
project\-local pair is read at the current working directory only.
.TP
\fB~/.claude.json, ~/.mcp.json, ./.mcp.json\fR
Claude Code MCP server definitions. Read, never written. The project\-local
file is read at the current working directory only.
.TP
\fB~/.claude/plugins/, ~/.claude/skills/\fR
Installed Claude Code plugins and skills. Vet reads each plugin.json and
skill.json manifest for its install source and integrity marker.
.TP
\fB~/.codex/config.toml, ~/.codex/hooks.json\fR
Codex CLI MCP server definitions and hooks. Read by vet through a scoped
reader that keeps only the launcher command, its arguments, and the endpoint
url.
.TP
\fB~/.cursor/mcp.json, ~/.cursor/hooks.json\fR
Cursor MCP server definitions and hooks. Read by vet.
.TP
\fB~/.copilot/mcp\-config.json, ~/.copilot/config.json, ~/.copilot/settings.json\fR
Copilot CLI MCP server definitions, application state, and user settings, all
resolved under $COPILOT_HOME when it is set, which replaces the default root
rather than layering over it. A declared root holding no Copilot store reads
exactly as an absent default one does: installation is decided by a config
file that was actually read, or by the session\-state and hooks markers under
whichever root is in force, never by the root's own existence. Read by vet.
From settings.json vet resolves whether CLI sessions are exported to GitHub.
remoteExport: false is the opt\-out GitHub documents as the way to stop
session syncing, remoteSessions: true forces export back on regardless of it,
and the documented default with neither set is sync on. remote is recorded as
a contributing key but never decides, because GitHub documents it two ways
that do not agree: the settings table says off keeps session data local only,
while the page on session syncing names remoteExport as the opt\-out and does
not mention remote at all. A config carrying only remote: off is therefore
reported as exporting, and the finding lists every key that was set, so a
reader who has decided the settings table is authoritative can see exactly
what skarn read and dismiss it. Where no settings.json exists at all, which is
the state a fresh install is in since neither the CLI nor its
\-\-no\-remote\-export flag writes one, the documented default is reported
against the path the file would occupy, so an untouched machine is not
mistaken for an opted\-out one. Comments are tolerated, since GitHub documents
JSONC for this file and the CLI writes a comment header into config.json
beside it. Read\-only; never written.
.TP
\fB~/.scout/m\-settings.json\fR
Microsoft Scout's permission grants, auto\-approve settings, and experiment
flags. Read by vet. The path has no platform branch. Scout resolves its config
root to ~/.scout on macOS and Windows alike.
.TP
\fB~/.scout/m\-mcp\-servers.json\fR
Microsoft Scout MCP server definitions. Read by vet. A user\-added server
nests its launcher or endpoint under a config object; a built\-in server has
none, because Scout generates its command at run time.
.TP
\fB~/.copilot/data.db\fR
The Copilot CLI's SQLite state store, which holds the signed\-in GitHub
account's access token and any bring\-your\-own\-key provider headers
alongside projects, workflows and review comments. Vet reads this file's POSIX
mode by a non\-following stat and never opens it: no byte of its contents is
read, the database is never opened as a database, so no token and no header
value can reach a finding and the only thing reported is whether the mode
grants group or other read; a symlinked store is reported as a coverage gap
rather than measured through its target, and a store whose owner\-read bit is
off is still measured. Its presence marks ~/.copilot as a live Copilot CLI
install for the Microsoft Scout residue rule, the same way session\-state/ and
hooks/ do, so the row is never skipped as residue. Never a scan source: the
session commands do not read it.
.TP
\fB~/Library/Application Support/Grok Bot/sand\-secrets.json\fR
Grok Bot's plaintext third\-party credential store, on macOS. Grok Bot is
xAI's always\-on desktop agents app, a different product from the Grok Build
CLI. Vet reads this file's POSIX mode by a non\-following stat and never opens
it: not one byte of its contents is read and the store is never parsed, so no
key name and no value from it can reach a finding and the only thing reported
is whether the mode grants group or other read; a symlinked store is reported
as a coverage gap rather than measured through its target. The path carries no
platform branch, so the store is vetted on macOS only; the Linux and Windows
locations are not read.
.TP
\fB~/.grokbot/settings.json\fR
Grok Bot's local\-exec daemon settings. Vet reads two declared posture flags
from this file, egressTunnelEnabled and webauthnProxyEnabled, and records only
whether each is present and true. A flag that is absent or false raises
nothing, so a default install stays silent.
.TP
\fB~/.grokbot/local\-exec\-daemon\-connection.json\fR
Grok Bot's local\-exec daemon connection state: the gateway base URL and the
bearer, network and VNC proxy tokens the cloud side uses to execute on this
machine. Vet treats the file as a presence signal and never parses it, so the
gateway URL and every token stay out of the process as well as out of every
output format. Its presence declares that the channel is configured, not that
a daemon is running.
.TP
\fB~/.gemini/settings.json\fR
Gemini CLI's user settings: MCP server definitions read by vet, and the hooks
object skarn setup \-\-agent gemini merges the guard into and doctor reads as
the user precedence layer.
.TP
\fB~/.grok/config.toml, ~/.grok/managed_config.toml, ~/.grok/requirements.toml\fR
Grok Build's user, managed, and requirements configuration layers. Read by vet
through a scoped TOML reader that collects MCP server launchers and endpoints,
the variable NAMES (never the values) of an MCP env table, the [ui]
permission_mode and yolo settings, allow\-action [permission] rules in both
the compact string\-array and the structured rules forms, hook handlers in
both TOML notations, and [[ui.notifications.hooks]] commands. Relocate the
whole directory with $GROK_HOME. Syntax the reader does not model, such as a
multiline string, is reported as a coverage gap naming the file and line
rather than skipped silently.
.TP
\fB/etc/grok/managed_config.toml, /etc/grok/requirements.toml\fR
Grok Build's system\-wide managed and requirements layers, deployed by an
organization. Read by vet exactly as the user layers are; SKARN_MANAGED_ROOT
prefixes both paths, so a staged payload can be vetted before it reaches a
machine.
.TP
\fB~/.grok/hooks/, ~/.grok/hooks\-paths\fR
Grok Build's global JSON hook directory and the registry file naming extra
hook sources. Vet reads the .json files one level down in the directory,
regular files only, and loads each ABSOLUTE line of the registry as a further
hook source; relative lines are ignored the way Grok ignores them, and a UNC
or //host/share target is recorded as a coverage gap and never opened. Both
are bounded per directory and per registry, and exceeding a bound is reported
rather than silently truncated.
.TP
\fB./.grok/config.toml, ./.grok/hooks/\fR
Grok Build's project\-scoped configuration and hook directory, contributing
MCP servers, plugins, and permission rules. Vet reads them at the current
working directory only, while Grok itself discovers a .grok/config.toml at
every level from the repository root down. Project hooks are collected
whatever the folder\-trust state, because a declared hook becomes live the
moment the folder is trusted, with no configuration change.
.TP
\fB%LOCALAPPDATA%\eMicrosoft\eCopilot\eUser Data\eCopilotCodingHome\e<profile>\emcp\-config.json, %LOCALAPPDATA%\eMicrosoft\eCopilot\eUser Data\eCopilotCodingHome\e<profile>\econfig.json, %LOCALAPPDATA%\eMicrosoft\eCopilot\eUser Data\eCopilotCodingHome\e<profile>\esettings.json (Windows)\fR
The configuration files of one Copilot in Code profile, read by vet. A profile
is a directory inside either CopilotCodingHome directory of %LOCALAPPDATA%,
the MSIX twin included, that holds session\-state, hooks or data.db, plus a
$COPILOT_HOME that names a directory inside a CopilotCodingHome directory. On
macOS and Linux only that $COPILOT_HOME reaches a profile. Such a
$COPILOT_HOME is never the Copilot CLI root, so the Copilot CLI rows read
~/.copilot instead. mcp\-config.json is the file the Copilot CLI documents
under $COPILOT_HOME, and a profile is Code's $COPILOT_HOME; the file was
absent on the verified install and the survey did not establish whether Code
reads it, so vet reports it as declared configuration only when it exists.
config.json is read with the comment header the app writes. From settings.json
vet reports only an export the file declares, remoteSessions: true. The sync
default GitHub documents is the Copilot CLI's, so a profile with no
settings.json, or one that sets neither remoteSessions nor remoteExport,
raises no finding. Vet does not read a profile's installed\-plugins or
extensions folder, a repository's .github\eextensions folder, the Copilot
app's own MCP configuration or the project folders under the Copilot projects
root, because neither their layout nor their file names were measured. A
profile folder that exists but cannot be opened is a coverage gap. A
CopilotCodingHome directory, or a $COPILOT_HOME profile, reached through a
link whose target is a network path is not opened and is a coverage gap named
NetworkPathRefused. Read\-only; never written. Verified against Copilot app
`156.0.4314.58` with GitHub Copilot CLI `1.0.93`.
.TP
\fB%LOCALAPPDATA%\eMicrosoft\eCopilot\eUser Data\eCopilotCodingHome\e<profile>\edata.db (Windows)\fR
The SQLite app database of one Copilot in Code profile; its accounts table has
an access_token column. Vet reads this file's POSIX mode by a non\-following
stat and never opens it: no byte of its contents is read, and the only thing
reported is whether the mode grants group or other read. A symlinked store is
reported as a coverage gap rather than measured through its target. Windows
has no POSIX mode, so on Windows vet counts the file as examined and reports
no mode finding. On macOS and Linux the finding applies to a copy of a profile
that a $COPILOT_HOME names. Vet reads none of its rows. Verified against
Copilot app `156.0.4314.58`.
.TP
\fB%LOCALAPPDATA%\eMicrosoft\eCopilot\eUser Data\eCopilotCodingHome\e<profile>\ehooks\e (Windows)\fR
The hook folder of one Copilot in Code profile, where skarn setup \-\-agent
copilot\-code writes skarn.json. Vet reads the .json files one level down,
regular files only, under the same bounds as the other hook directories, and
reports each command, bash and powershell field of a hook entry, flat or
nested, at its own locator, for example hooks.preToolUse[0].powershell.
Copilot in Code loaded a hook file from this folder under any file name. When
the folder, or a folder on the way to it, is a link whose target is a network
path, vet opens nothing through it and reports a coverage gap named
NetworkPathRefused on the folder. Verified against Copilot app `156.0.4314.58`
with GitHub Copilot CLI `1.0.93`.
.TP
\fB~/.copilot/hooks/\fR
The Copilot CLI's personal hook folder, under $COPILOT_HOME when it is set and
does not name a Copilot in Code profile. Vet reads the .json files one level
down, regular files only, and reports each command, bash and powershell field
of a hook entry. On a Microsoft Scout machine whose ~/.copilot is only residue
the row is skipped, as the other ~/.copilot rows are.
.TP
\fB./.github/hooks/\fR
The working directory's Copilot hook folder, which the Copilot CLI and the
Copilot cloud agent read, and which Copilot in Code read in a folder project.
Vet reads the .json files one level down, regular files only, at the current
working directory only, and reports each command, bash and powershell field of
a hook entry. Vet reads it whatever the project type; Copilot in Code reading
it in a repository project was not measured.
